[?2004h[01;32mappuser@2cc695c72b41[00m:[01;34m/app[00m$ printf '%s\n' '--- review_protocol.md ---'; nl -ba /app/input/review_protocol.md | sed -n '1,220p'
[?2004l--- review_protocol.md ---
     1	# Review protocol — letter to opposing counsel
     2	
     3	Governs every line in `letter_lines.csv`, weighed against `original_instruction.md` and
     4	`clarification.md`.
     5	
     6	## RP-401 Which record prevails
     7	
     8	On any subject the clarification speaks to, the clarification prevails. If the original instruction and the clarification take different positions on a subject, the clarification wins and the original falls away, no matter which entry the draft cites or how plainly the original was put: what the client said last is what the client instructs. For a line on a subject the clarification speaks to, the governing entry is always the clarification entry and never the original one (`RP-401`).
     9	
    10	## RP-402 Subjects the clarification repeats
    11	
    12	If the clarification repeats a subject in the very terms the original used, the clarification entry nonetheless governs the line, and the line is weighed against it (`RP-402`).
    13	
    14	## RP-403 Subjects only the original speaks to
    15	
    16	If the clarification leaves a subject alone, the original entry on that subject governs and the line is weighed against it (`RP-403`).
    17	
    18	## RP-404 Subjects neither record speaks to
    19	
    20	If neither record speaks to a subject, nothing governs: the line's verdict is NOT_IN_RECORD and its record entry is `NONE`, however reasonable the line may read (`RP-404`). A cited_entry that exists nowhere does not by itself make a line NOT_IN_RECORD — the governing entry is determined by the line's subject under RP-401 and RP-403, not by the cited_entry. NOT_IN_RECORD applies only when the subject itself is one that neither the original instruction nor the clarification addresses.
    21	
    22	## RP-405 How a line is weighed
    23	
    24	Weighed against its governing entry, a line is VERIFIED when the position it takes is the entry's own, and AT_ODDS when the entry's position differs from it — the reverse, something narrower, or a different person (`RP-405`).
    25	
    26	## RP-406 The review table
    27	
    28	`record_entry` holds the id of the entry that governs the line, spelled as the records spell it, or `NONE` (`RP-406`).
    29	
    30	
    31	## RP-407 Cross-subject citation exception
    32	
    33	Notwithstanding RP-401, when a line's `cited_entry` is a clarification entry (CL-…) that speaks to a subject different from the line's own `subject`, the original instruction entry for the line's `subject` governs — not the clarification entry for the line's subject. The line is weighed against that original instruction entry, and the `record_entry` column reports the original instruction entry, not the clarification. (RP-407)
    34	
    35	This applies only when the cited_entry is a clarification entry for a *different* subject. When the cited_entry is a clarification entry for the *same* subject, RP-401 applies normally. When the cited_entry is an original entry or NONE, RP-401 and RP-403 apply as before.
    36	
    37	## RP-408 Compound positions
    38	
    39	When a governing entry's position has two or more required limbs (for example, a marking and a tone requirement), a line that asserts only one limb and drops the others takes something narrower than the entry's own position, and is AT_ODDS (`RP-408`). The line is VERIFIED only when it takes the entry's full position, including every required limb.
    40	
    41	## Figures
    42	
    43	`verified_count` is the number of lines whose verdict is VERIFIED.
    44	`at_odds_count` is the at odds count: the lines whose verdict is AT_ODDS.
    45	`not_in_record_count` is the number of lines whose verdict is NOT_IN_RECORD.
    46	`clarification_governed_count` is the number of lines whose record entry is a clarification entry (`CL-…`).
    47	In the answer, `Letter lines at odds with the record` is the number of lines whose governing entry takes a position
    48	other than the line's.
[?2004h[01;32mappuser@2cc695c72b41[00m:[01;34m/app[00m$ printf '%s\n' '--- clarification.md ---'; nl -ba /app/input/clarification.md | sed -n '1,220p'
[?2004l--- clarification.md ---
     1	# Clarification — the client's later message
     2	
     3	What the client added afterwards, one entry per subject. The subject shown on each entry is the value the draft's `subject` column uses; any subject missing here is one the clarification leaves alone.
     4	
     5	## CL-301 `addressee`
     6	
     7	To be clear: Adam is the father. The letter goes to Catherine's attorney — the mother's attorney — not to Adam's.
     8	
     9	## CL-302 `request`
    10	
    11	The request stands: neither child overnight in her care unless the father is present.
    12	
    13	## CL-303 `enclosure`
    14	
    15	Yes, enclose the certified copy of the custodial plan.
    16	
    17	## CL-304 `salutation`
    18	
    19	Open with Dear Counsel, since the attorney's name is still to be confirmed.
    20	
    21	## CL-305 `copies`
    22	
    23	Do not copy anyone on it.
    24	
    25	## CL-306 `delivery`
    26	
    27	Send it by post to counsel's office.
    28	
    29	## CL-307 `tone`
    30	
    31	Use a firm, formal and brief tone — not soft, and not long-winded.
    32	
    33	## CL-308 `behavioural_plan`
    34	
    35	The plan is about confidence, stability and peer relations. The stays have set back confidence only — not the whole plan.
    36	
    37	## CL-309 `concern`
    38	
    39	I am concerned only about the position she has been put in. Do not raise whether she appreciates that the stays violate the order.
    40	
    41	## CL-310 `interim_contact`
    42	
    43	Interim contact arrangements must address all three aspects of the behavioural plan together — confidence, stability and peer relations — because interim contact affects all three. This entry takes precedence over CL-308 for any line whose subject is interim_contact, even when the line's wording addresses the behavioural plan or the stays.
    44	
    45	## CL-311 `without_prejudice`
    46	
    47	Lines marked 'without prejudice' are settlement offers and must use a soft, conciliatory tone — the opposite of the firm tone the clarification requires for the rest of the letter. This entry takes precedence over CL-307 for any line whose subject is without_prejudice, even when the line's wording addresses the tone of the letter.
